{% extends '@CitadelAureum/core/docs/layout.html.twig' %}

{% block article %}
    <p>
        Aureum stores guest details, so it comes with the controls needed to
        stay on the right side of data-protection rules: retention policies,
        anonymisation, and an access log.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention policies</h2>
    <p>
        Under <strong>Manager → Data Retention</strong>, set how many months
        guest details are kept per module (between 1 and 240). When a record
        passes its retention period, the guest's personal details are
        anonymised while the operational record itself is kept for reporting.
        Leaving a module blank keeps its details indefinitely, so set a policy
        deliberately rather than by default.
    </p>

    <h2>Access log</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Manager → Access Log</strong> records who viewed and changed
        what. Use it to answer "who looked at this guest's details?", and
        expect it to be checked during audits.
    </p>

    <div class="au-docs-note">
        <i class="ph ph-info"></i>
        <p>
            Collect the minimum: if a booking doesn't need a phone number,
            don't record one. Retention policies can shorten what you keep,
            but the best data protection is data you never stored.
        </p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
